---
// src/pages/guides/landscape-design.astro
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const businessName = import.meta.env['PUBLIC_BUSINESS_NAME'] || 'Palm & Agave Nursery';
---

<Layout
  title="Using Agaves and Palms Together in Florida Landscapes — Plant Guides | Palm & Agave Nursery"
  description="How to combine architectural agaves and vertical palms in contemporary, tropical, and minimal Florida landscape designs — with guidance on spacing, scale, and plant combinations that work in Hillsborough County."
>
  <Header cartCount={0} />

  <div class="guide-page">
    <article class="guide-article container">
      <header class="guide-header">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol class="breadcrumb-list">
            <li><a href="/" class="breadcrumb-link">Home</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li><a href="/guides" class="breadcrumb-link">Guides</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li class="breadcrumb-current" aria-current="page">Landscape Design</li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Landscape Design</p>
        <h1 class="guide-title">Using agaves and palms together in Florida landscapes</h1>
        <p class="guide-meta">Agaves and palms can work together beautifully when their forms, scales, and needs are understood. This guide covers how to combine them with intention rather than just placing them side by side.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="guide-content">
        <p class="guide-intro">
          Agaves and palms are a natural pairing in many Florida landscapes because they offer different kinds of structure. Agaves contribute low, architectural rosettes with strong outlines. Palms contribute vertical form, crown presence, and a sense of height and movement. Together, they can create landscapes that feel layered, intentional, and distinctively Floridian.
        </p>
        <p>
          The key is to combine them with purpose — matching plant size, form, sun exposure, and scale to the space and the design.
        </p>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Think in layers</h2>
          <p>
            A strong landscape usually has more than one layer of plant form. Agaves and palms each occupy a different place in that layering.
          </p>
          <p>
            Agaves tend to work as mid-level or low-level structure. Their rosettes read clearly from a distance and from nearby. Palms tend to work as vertical structure — a focal point, a frame, a screen, or a canopy element.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you combine them, think about what each plant is doing in the composition. Is the palm the vertical anchor? Are the agaves the architectural ground layer? Are the agaves repeating a shape that the palm echoes at a different scale?
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Match scale intentionally</h2>
          <p>
            One of the most common mistakes is pairing a small agave with a palm that will eventually overwhelm it, or placing a large specimen agave where it competes with a palm for the same visual space.
          </p>
          <p>
            Consider the mature size of both plants, not just the size at purchase. A compact agave in a 10-gallon container may remain small for years, while a palm planted nearby may be growing toward 20 or 30 feet. That can be a good combination if the design is planned for it. It can be a poor one if the agave is meant to be a permanent feature in the palm's shadow.
          </p>
          <p>
            Conversely, a large specimen agave placed at the base of a slender palm can create a strong, intentional composition if the sizes and forms are balanced.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Contemporary and minimal landscapes</h2>
          <p>
            Agaves and palms both work well in contemporary and minimal designs because each can stand on its own form. In these landscapes, the goal is often clean lines, restraint, and strong individual plants rather than dense planting.
          </p>
          <p>
            Useful approaches include:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>A single specimen palm as the vertical anchor, with one or a few agaves spaced deliberately around it</li>
            <li>Agaves repeated in a row or grid for rhythm, with a palm or two as vertical punctuation</li>
            <li>A large container palm paired with a smaller container agave for a controlled, movable composition</li>
            <li>A clean hardscape base with agaves and palms placed as sculptural elements rather than filler</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            In these designs, spacing and negative space matter as much as the plants themselves. Do not feel compelled to fill every gap.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Tropical and relaxed landscapes</h2>
          <p>
            Palms naturally read as tropical or subtropical, and agaves can add a contrasting architectural layer without making the design feel rigid. In a more relaxed tropical landscape, the combination can feel lush, layered, and organic.
          </p>
          <p>
            Useful approaches include:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>A palm as the main vertical element, with agaves tucked around the base or flanking the trunk</li>
            <li>Multiple palms of varying heights with agaves repeating in the understory</li>
            <li>Agaves used as structural punctuation between more soft-leafed tropical plants</li>
            <li>Specimen palms with a single agave or a small group of agaves as a deliberate ground feature</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            In these landscapes, variation in form and texture is welcome. The goal is not minimalism but balance — enough contrast to stay interesting, enough repetition to stay cohesive.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Spacing and clearance</h2>
          <p>
            Give plants room to exist as they mature. This is especially important with palms, which can become substantial over time, and with agaves, which can spread to their mature rosette size.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before planting, consider:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>How much space the palm's crown will need at maturity</li>
            <li>How much space the agave's rosette will eventually occupy</li>
            <li>Whether the agave will be shaded out by the palm as both grow</li>
            <li>Clearance for walkways, driveways, doors, and sight lines</li>
            <li>Maintenance access for pruning, removal of dead fronds, and inspection</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If a palm will cast significant shade, make sure the agaves placed near it can tolerate that level of light. If an agave needs full sun, do not plant it where the palm will shade it for most of the day in a few years.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Repetition and rhythm</h2>
          <p>
            Repetition creates rhythm. A row of identical agaves can be striking on its own. A row of palms can create a strong corridor or entry sequence. Combining the two can create a layered rhythm if done with care.
          </p>
          <p>
            Examples of intentional repetition:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Agaves spaced at regular intervals along a path or entry, with a palm at the end as a terminus</li>
            <li>A double row of palms with agaves filling the space between</li>
            <li>A coral or courtyard layout where palms define the corners and agaves soften the edges</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Repetition works best when the plants are actually repeated correctly — same species, similar size, similar spacing — rather than mixed randomly.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Practical reminders</h2>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Match sun exposure across the planting. If agaves need full sun, do not tuck them into the palm's future shade unless the design intends that and the agaves can tolerate it.</li>
            <li>Match drainage. Both agaves and many palms dislike wet feet. If the site drains poorly, improve it or choose plants and placements accordingly.</li>
            <li>Plan for maintenance access. Palms need frond care and inspection. Agaves need occasional dead-leaf removal and safe placement.</li>
            <li>Respect mature size. A design that looks balanced at planting can become crowded or imbalanced later if mature sizes are ignored.</li>
            <li>Be intentional about spine hazards. If the design puts agaves near paths, choose smooth-leafed species or place spiny ones carefully.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section guide-section--cta">
          <p class="guide-cta-text">
            Ready to plan a landscape that combines agaves and palms? Browse our catalogs, filter by the attributes that matter for your design, and contact us if you need help matching plants to a specific plan.
          </p>
          <div class="guide-cta-links">
            <a href="/shop/agaves" class="btn btn-primary">Shop Agaves</a>
            <a href="/shop/palms" class="btn btn-outline">Shop Palm Trees</a>
            <a href="/wholesale/project-inquiry" class="btn btn-outline">Project Inquiry</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .guide-page { padding: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-16); }
  .guide-article { max-width: 760px; }

  .breadcrumb { margin-bottom: var(--space-8); padding-bottom: var(--space-5); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .breadcrumb-list { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  .breadcrumb-link { color: var(--color-text-secondary); text-decoration: none; transition: color var(--transition-fast); }
  .breadcrumb-link:hover, .breadcrumb-link:focus-visible { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .breadcrumb-current { color: var(--color-text-primary); font-weight: var(--font-medium); }

  .guide-eyebrow { font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: var(--font-semibold); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.12em; color: var(--color-sage); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .guide-title { font-size: clamp(var(--text-2xl), 4vw, var(--text-4xl)); color: var(--color-green-deep); line-height: 1.15; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .guide-meta { font-size: var(--text-base); color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 600px; line-height: 1.6; }

  .guide-content { margin-top: var(--space-10); }
  .guide-intro { font-size: var(--text-lg); line-height: 1.75; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }

  .guide-section { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .guide-section-heading { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-xl); color: var(--color-green-deep); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .guide-section p { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.75; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }

  .guide-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .guide-list li { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-secondary); padding-left: var(--space-5); position: relative; }
  .guide-list li::before { content: '—'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-sage); }

  .guide-section--cta { padding: var(--space-8); background-color: rgba(41,79,59,0.04); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); }
  .guide-cta-text { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .guide-cta-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-3); }
</style>
